This is where I'd change direction a little.

So far we've been building **infrastructure**:

* Page model
* Partial templates
* HTMX
* Keyboard navigation

Those are foundations.

Now I'd start building the **review workflow** itself.

## My recommended roadmap

I would **not** add Favorites, Delete, Rotate, etc. next.

Instead, I'd make the reviewer **fast**.

The biggest performance issue in the current code is that every click does this:

```text
Arrow →
    ↓
GET /review/content
    ↓
Scan()
    ↓
Walk filesystem
    ↓
Decode images
    ↓
Return page
```

Even if it feels okay now, it's unnecessary work.

---

# Phase 6 — Review Session

This is the first feature I'd implement.

Instead of rescanning the filesystem on every request:

```
Review Session

ID: ab83f9

Images
    0
    1
    2
    3
    ...

Current = 17

MinSize = 200
```

Navigation becomes

```
Arrow →
      ↓
Current++
      ↓
Return image 18
```

No scan.

No directory walk.

Nearly instant.

---

## Why this matters

Right now:

```
500 images

Arrow Right

Scan 500 files

Arrow Right

Scan 500 files

Arrow Right

Scan 500 files
```

With a session:

```
500 images

Arrow Right

index++

Arrow Right

index++

Arrow Right

index++
```

Huge difference.

---

# This also unlocks...

Once a session exists, every future feature becomes easier.

## Delete

```
Delete

remove Images[current]

show Images[current]
```

No rescan.

---

## Keep

```
Keep

Current++
```

---

## Favorite

```
Images[current].Favorite = true
```

---

## Undo

```
History stack

Undo
```

---

## Random

```
Current = rand.Intn(...)
```

---

## Slideshow

```
Current++

every 3 seconds
```

---

# I'd introduce a new package

```
internal/

reviewsession/

    session.go
    store.go
```

Very small.

```
type Session struct {

    Images []Image

    Current int

    MinSize int

}
```

---

Then

```
type Store struct {

    sessions map[string]*Session

}
```

About 100 lines of Go.

